WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.950
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:11.710
Today, through the power of AI research, we
are going to see how easily we can ask virtual

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:18.740
characters to not only say what we say, but
we can even become an art director and ask

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:34.340
them to add some emotion 

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.460
to it.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.790
So, how does this happen?

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:48.850
Well, in goes what we say, for instance, me
uttering Dear Fellow Scholars, or anything

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:49.850
else.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:55.620
And, here is the key, we can also specify
the emotional state of the character.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.700
And this AI does the rest.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.760
That is absolutely amazing.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.770
But, it gets even more amazing.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.550
Now, hold on to your papers, and, look.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:15.740
Yes, that’s right, this was possible back
in 2017, approximately 400 Two Minute Papers

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.330
episodes ago.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:22.200
And whenever I showcase results like this,
I always get the question from you Fellow

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:28.990
Scholars, asking that “yes, this all looks
great, but when do I get to use this?”.

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:31.590
And the answer is, right now.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:32.590
Why?

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:39.220
Because NVIDIA has released Audio2Face, a
collection of AI techniques that we can use

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.460
to perform this quickly and easily.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:49.870
Look, we can record our voice live, and have
a virtual character say what we are saying.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:55.040
But, it does not stop there, it also has 3
amazing features.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:03.200
One, we can even perform a face swap, not
only between humanoid characters, but, my

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.200
goodness.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.000
Even from a humanoid to, for instance, a Rhino!

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.700
Now that’s something.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:10.930
I love it.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.950
But wait, there is more.

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:17.230
There is this.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.610
And this too.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:28.030
Two, we can still specify emotions, like anger,
sadness and excitement, and the virtual character

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.670
will perform that for us.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:34.780
We only need to provide our voice, no more
acting skills are required.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:41.370
In my opinion, this will be a godsend in any
kind of digital media, computer games, or

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.630
even when meeting our friends in a virtual
space.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:50.420
Three, the usability of this technique is
out of this world.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:56.709
For instance, it does not eat a great deal
of resources, so we can run multiple instances

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.420
of it at the same time.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:05.160
This is a wonderful usability feature, one
of many that really makes or breaks when it

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.300
comes to a new technique being used in the
industry or not.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.440
An aspect not to be underestimated.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:18.680
And here is another usability feature: it
works well with Unreal Engine's MetaHuman,

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.780
this is a piece of software that can create
virtual humans.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:29.110
And with that, we can not only create these
virtual humans, but become the voice actors

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:34.270
for them without having to hire a bunch of
animators.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:36.280
How cool is that.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:42.489
Now I believe this is an earlier version of
MetaHuman, here is the newer one.

00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:45.120
Wow, way better.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.010
Just imagine how cool it would be to voice
these characters automatically.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:57.349
Now, the important lesson is that this was
possible in a paper in 2017, and now, in a

00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:03.959
few years it has vastly improved, so much
so that it is now out there deployed in a

00:04:03.959 --> 00:04:07.450
real product that we can use right now.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.739
That is a powerful democratizing force for
computer animation.

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:16.269
So, yes, the papers that you see here are
real.

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:22.629
As real as it gets, and this tech transfer
can often occur in just a few years time.

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:25.979
In some other cases, even quicker.

00:04:25.979 --> 00:04:27.639
What a time to be alive!

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:30.129
So, what would you use this for?

00:04:30.129 --> 00:04:33.169
I’d love to know what you think.

00:04:33.169 --> 00:05:32.139
Let me know in the comments below!

00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:35.080
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

